findings.body:
The PR removes `useRouter` from the `next/navigation` import and deletes the `const router = useRouter();` line in `BookerWebWrapperComponent`. The file still calls `router.push(...)` at lines 193, 228, 232, and 238, and `[router]` is still the dependency array at line 195. At runtime this throws `ReferenceError: router is not defined` on the first call to those handlers. Because the Booker wrapper is used by the team, user, dynamic-link, org instant-meeting, and embed booking pages, every one of those surfaces is affected.

::diff{file="apps/web/modules/bookings/components/BookerWebWrapper.tsx" lines="1-40"}

::callout{type="warning"}
This is a stale-reference bug from the cleanup of unused imports. Unused-import cleanup is correct only when the symbol is truly unused. `grep` for `router` in the file shows it is still used.

::suggestion{file="apps/web/modules/bookings/components/BookerWebWrapper.tsx" lines="4-4"}
import { useSearchParams, usePathname, useRouter } from "next/navigation";

Also restore `const router = useRouter();` at the top of `BookerWebWrapperComponent`, next to `pathname` and `searchParams`.

When `isRRWeightsEnabled` is true and two hosts have the same weight, the original `sortHosts` returned `priorityB - priorityA` (higher priority first). The moved version returns `priorityA - priorityB`, which reverses that order. The non-weights branch is unchanged, so the bug only shows up when round-robin weights are enabled and hosts tie on weight.

The function is called from `EventTeamAssignmentTab.tsx` (lines 324 and 332), `HostEditDialogs.tsx` (lines 82 and 178), and `useEventTypeForm.ts` line 103, which sorts `eventType.hosts` when the form loads. That last one means the host order saved in the form changes as soon as the editor opens. Nothing in the diff says this change is intentional, and the PR description only says the function was moved.

::diff{file="packages/lib/bookings/hostGroupUtils.ts" lines="41-56"}

::suggestion{file="packages/lib/bookings/hostGroupUtils.ts" lines="52-54"}
    if (weightA === weightB) {
      return priorityB - priorityA;
    } else {
      return weightB - weightA;
    }

Keep the function body identical to the original. If the reversal is intended, document it and cover it with a test.

The PR changes runtime behavior of `sortHosts` (see the comparator finding) and relocates six wrappers that participate in routing and form state. I found no test in `packages/lib/bookings` or elsewhere that covers `sortHosts` or `hostGroupUtils`. The PR's verification step is `yarn type-check:ci` plus a manual page check. That would not have caught the `router` ReferenceError or the flipped comparator, because a type-check only flags undefined names and the comparator is valid TypeScript.

A small unit test on `sortHosts` for the three branches (weights disabled, weights enabled with unequal weights, weights enabled with equal weights and differing priorities) would have caught the regression immediately.

::diff{file="packages/lib/bookings/hostGroupUtils.ts" lines="41-56"}

The `setup` and `availability` entries in `tabMap` were reindented from the surrounding 6-space style to 8-space style with 4-space JSX attributes. The rest of the file uses the 6-space form, so this will fail the project's Prettier/Biome check and makes the diff noisier than it needs to be. The change does nothing functional.

::diff{file="apps/web/modules/event-types/components/EventTypeWebWrapper.tsx" lines="234-251"}

Revert these two JSX blocks to the same indentation used by the `team` entry below them.

The diff rewrites `EventLimitsTabWebWrapper.tsx` and `EventRecurringWebWrapper.tsx` so that their final line has no trailing newline. The content is the same, but most linters and Git tooling treat a missing EOF newline as an error, and this is an unnecessary change in a move that should be a pure rename.

::diff{file="apps/web/modules/event-types/components/tabs/limits/EventLimitsTabWebWrapper.tsx" lines="5-8"}

Add the trailing newline back so the file ends with a single newline, matching the original.

The PR's aim is to let WebWrapper code in `apps/web` import sibling modules. But `EventTypeWebWrapper.tsx` now imports `useEventTypeForm`, `useHandleRouteChange`, and `useTabsNavigations` through `@calcom/atoms/event-types/hooks/...`, so the web app depends on the atoms package for hooks that live there. The PR adds those hook exports to the atoms `package.json` to make this work.

This is a reasonable intermediate step, since the hooks are still referenced by the platform atoms. It does mean the dependency still points from `apps/web` into `packages/platform/atoms`, and that the moved wrapper is not fully self-contained. The PR description says the hooks are still a later move, so this is a known trade-off rather than a defect. Note it for the follow-up refactor.

